pr_comments:
The ectest failure seems to have uncovered a latent bug in the EC library. It seems the "asn1_flag" setting was defaulting to "named curve", and you have to explicitly change it if you have set up an explicit curve. It seems to me that the default should be the other way around, and that we can change the "asn1_flag" when we know what the group name is.

The following patch fixes the test failure:

````
diff --git a/crypto/ec/ec_lib.c b/crypto/ec/ec_lib.c
index e1b92f7c33..508c96fef5 100644
--- a/crypto/ec/ec_lib.c
+++ b/crypto/ec/ec_lib.c
@@ -63,7 +63,7 @@ EC_GROUP *ec_group_new_ex(OSSL_LIB_CTX *libctx, const char *propq,
         if (ret->cofactor == NULL)
             goto err;
     }
-    ret->asn1_flag = OPENSSL_EC_NAMED_CURVE;
+    ret->asn1_flag = OPENSSL_EC_EXPLICIT_CURVE;
     ret->asn1_form = POINT_CONVERSION_UNCOMPRESSED;
     if (!meth->group_init(ret))
         goto err;
@@ -481,6 +481,7 @@ const BIGNUM *EC_GROUP_get0_cofactor(const EC_GROUP *group)
 void EC_GROUP_set_curve_name(EC_GROUP *group, int nid)
 {
     group->curve_name = nid;
+    group->asn1_flag = OPENSSL_EC_NAMED_CURVE;
 }
 
 int EC_GROUP_get_curve_name(const EC_GROUP *group)
````
Basically the test was creating an explicit params group, but it was still marked as using named group encoding. This was failing when it was being exported to the provider because the provider didn't have a named group that matched the explicit params.

An alternative fix would be to change the test to explicitly call `EC_GROUP_set_asn1_flag()` on the constructed group (`altgroup`).
